{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "P1A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6 and 9 distinguish task/scoring documentation from reliability evidence. Reinterpreting test-retest as repeated evaluation of a fixed AI, execution history is unknown; §9.8 is 0 (insufficient information), not evidence of instability.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "gives no execution history",
          "reason": "The passage does not establish whether repeated evaluations occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "one headline score of 75%",
          "reason": "One reported score provides no evidence of variation across identical evaluations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P1B",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §9, repeated-evaluation evidence is explicitly absent. Reinterpreting test-retest for a fixed AI, §9.8 is 0; absence of repeats establishes neither stability nor instability.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "no repeat evaluations were conducted",
          "reason": "The execution record explicitly excludes the claimed repeated evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "this was the only evaluation performed",
          "reason": "A single evaluation cannot demonstrate variation across identical evaluations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9 supports examining repeatability under controlled conditions, reinterpreted here for an unchanged AI. The observed range is two percentage points and meets the report's rule. This local rule is not an EFPA reliability coefficient or evidence of precision beyond this comparison.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly describes repeated evaluations under the same conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "73, 74, 74, 75, 74 and 73 percent",
          "reason": "The two-point range is within the stated maximum of three percentage points."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2B",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §9, the repeated evaluations provide direct evidence of score variation for this AI and setup. The 31-point range fails the report's three-point rule. This supports a comparison-specific precision concern without supplying an EFPA reliability coefficient.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly holds evaluation conditions constant."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "51, 66, 79, 55, 82 and 62 percent",
          "reason": "The 31-point range exceeds the permitted three percentage points."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.2–6.3 separate documentation from evidence about instrument quality. Topic and scoring descriptions establish neither answer accuracy nor repeatability. Under the AI reinterpretation of §9, reliability information is insufficient; answer quality under §§6.1 and 7 remains unknown.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It gives no execution history",
          "reason": "The supplied material does not establish that repeated evaluations were studied."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no findings about mistakes in the provided answers",
          "reason": "No answer errors are demonstrated; their presence or absence remains unknown."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3B",
      "instrument_review": "The appendix adds information relevant to EFPA §6.3.8 on software requirements and operation. It does not add §9 repeatability evidence or §§6.1 and 7 answer-quality findings. Better technical documentation does not itself establish measurement quality.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It reports no further evaluation results",
          "reason": "Installation information supplies no evidence that identical-condition repeats were studied."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no further evaluation results or examination of answers",
          "reason": "The appendix does not demonstrate mistakes in supplied answers."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.1 recognizes the independent editorial examination as development evidence, distinct from demonstrated item correctness. Reconciliation supplies no quantified pre-reconciliation agreement. Editorial checking is not automatically an EFPA §9.6 study of response-scoring reliability.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release",
          "reason": "The passage describes development-stage checking of both questions and answers."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "does not state whether mistakes were found or how many remained",
          "reason": "The review process does not establish that every released answer is correct."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4B",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §§6.1 and 7, editorial examination is documented but released answer errors are also explicitly documented. These are separate findings. The missing pre-reconciliation agreement count prevents assessment of editorial agreement; its relevance to response-scoring reliability is not established.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release",
          "reason": "Both components underwent development-stage examination."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "twelve supplied answers as wrong in the released version",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly identifies released answers that are incorrect."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.3.2 asks for evidence that automated scoring works correctly; deterministic marking establishes consistency for identical text, not accuracy. Under §9.6, inter-rater reliability is inapplicable to the described fixed rule without judgemental scoring.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No comparison of those marks with independently established correct marks is reported",
          "reason": "The passage leaves such testing unreported rather than establishing whether it occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "identical text always receives the same mark",
          "reason": "Consistent marks need not be correct, and no universal accuracy evidence is supplied."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5B",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §6.3.2, comparison against independently correct marks provides a scoring check, with two unresolved discrepancies showing an accuracy problem in the tested responses. Determinism does not resolve that problem. EFPA §9.6 inter-rater reliability is inapplicable to the described fixed scoring rule.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "tested the software against independently established correct marks for 20 chosen responses",
          "reason": "The claimed independent comparison is explicitly reported."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Two marks differed and the discrepancies remain unresolved",
          "reason": "Disagreements with independently correct marks conflict with correctness for every possible response."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §10 evaluates evidence against the stated score use. Reinterpreting examinees as AI systems, the counts support this narrowly defined comparison. Under §8.3.2.2, norms are inapplicable to this sole raw-score ranking purpose; broader generalization is not supported.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identifying which system solved more of these 48 grid problems in this evaluation",
          "reason": "The reported counts directly answer the stated, limited comparison."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24",
          "reason": "A's reported count exceeds B's under the specified conditions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §10 requires validity evidence for the intended interpretation. Reinterpreting examinees as AI systems, the grid-task counts support a local comparison but do not establish general intelligence across subjects and settings. Broader content coverage and generalization evidence remain unknown.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No results from other tasks or settings are supplied",
          "reason": "The supplied evidence does not justify the stated cross-subject and real-world interpretation."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24",
          "reason": "The observed grid-task comparison favors A regardless of the broader intended use."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§3, 6.1.3.1 and 6.3.2 relate scoring design to measurement aims. The binary rule matches the explicitly stated requirement. Lack of partial credit is not a defect relative to that requirement; broader validity and precision remain unestablished.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "count only completely correct output grids",
          "reason": "Awarding one point only for a completely correct grid implements the stated requirement."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "partial correctness is deliberately outside this comparison",
          "reason": "Zero credit for partial correctness follows the stated requirement."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§3, 6.1.3.1 and 6.3.2 identify a direct mismatch between scoring implementation and its stated aim. Zero credit for partially correct grids conflicts with required cell-level credit. This is an evidenced scoring-design problem, not merely missing documentation.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "give a response credit for every correctly completed cell",
          "reason": "The binary rule withholds credit for correct cells within partially correct grids."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "including partially correct output grids",
          "reason": "The stated requirement explicitly includes credit that the rule withholds."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9 distinguishes identical-condition repeatability from changes in administration. Reinterpreted for AI evaluation, this study examines instruction variation, not identical-instruction repeats. It supplies no equivalence coefficient or basis for an EFPA precision rating.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No evaluation repeats an identical instruction",
          "reason": "Instructions change, so the evaluations do not have identical conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "each evaluation uses a differently worded instruction",
          "reason": "The study reports score changes while varying instructions and holding the listed other conditions fixed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8B",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §9, the study addresses repeated-evaluation variation for an unchanged AI and setup. It does not examine instruction sensitivity. Without score values or precision estimates, the degree of repeatability remains unknown.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "settings and instruction are identical on every evaluation",
          "reason": "The study reports score changes across evaluations with the specified conditions held constant."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No different instruction or presentation is studied",
          "reason": "The study explicitly excludes instruction variation."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "No task instruction required clarification. The complete original EFPA 2025 framework was retained: Part A §§1–5 description, Part B §§6–12 evaluation and final recommendations, and Part C sources. Only the supplied passages and EFPA extraction were used. AI systems replace human test takers only where explicitly discussed; human bodily capabilities, demographic classifications, clinical uses and debriefing are inapplicable as literal questions about these AI subjects. Human stakeholder accessibility and fairness remain separate, generally undocumented issues. Unaddressed documentation, materials, norms, reliability, validity, fair-use support and digital-report properties remain unknown unless explicitly inapplicable; missing report evidence does not establish that reports do not exist. No report samples were supplied for §12 review. EFPA rating 0 means insufficient information, not demonstrated inadequacy; claim labels are separate from EFPA ratings. Where a critical attribute receives 0 or 1, EFPA recommends research or exceptional expert use; these brief cases do not establish unrestricted suitability. No aggregate benchmark-quality score was calculated. Source: EFPA (2025), Test Review Model v5.0, supplied extraction, https://www.efpa.eu/, CC BY-SA 4.0. The authoritative PDF was not independently inspected, and ambiguous extraction anchors were not needed."
}
